                                </p>
                                
                                <p align="center">
                                    <a href="https://www.arturo-lang.io"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/language-Arturo-6A156B.svg?&style=flat&colorA=000000&colorB=000000" alt="Language: Arturo"/></a>
                                    &nbsp;
                                    <a href="https://github.com/RickBarretto/pkgr.sql.art/blob/main/LICENSE"><img src="https://img.shields.io/github/license/RickBarretto/pkgr.sql.art?&style=flat&colorA=000000&colorB=000000" alt="License"/></a>
                                    &nbsp;
                                    <a href="https://github.com/RickBarretto/pkgr.sql.art/actions"><img src="https://img.shields.io/github/actions/workflow/status/RickBarretto/pkgr.sql.art/test.yml?branch=main&style=flat&colorA=000000&colorB=000000" alt="CI Status"/></a>
                                </p>
                                
                                Build SQL with Arturo blocks, compile it for SQLite or PostgreSQL, and keep
                                SQL text separate from parameter values.
                                
                                - **Query:** filters, joins, aggregates, subqueries, CTEs, and set operations.
                                - **Write:** insert, update, and delete rows; define tables, views, and triggers.
                                - **Inspect:** build a statement once, then choose a dialect when compiling.
                                
                                > [!IMPORTANT]
                                > Requires **Arturo 0.10.0+**. SQLite execution is included. For PostgreSQL,
                                > compile the statement and pass the output to your own driver.
                                
                                [Quick start](#quick-start) · [Run a query](#run-a-query) ·
                                [Documentation](#documentation) · [Development](#development)
                                
                                ## At a glance
                                
                                <p align="center">
                                    <img src="./docs/images/terminal.png" width="720" alt="Arturo SQL query filtering programming languages released since 2000, with Arturo, Rust and Go displayed in a tabular terminal table." />
                                </p>
                                
                                Try this SQLite example with `arturo examples/languages.art`.
                                It queries programming languages and formats the results with `tabular`.
                                
                                ## Installation
                                
                                ```sh
                                arturo -p install sql
                                ```
                                
                                ## Command-line interface
                                
                                The `sql` CLI lets you execute Arturo SQL DSL statements against SQLite databases or compile them into SQL without establishing a database connection.
                                
                                ### Usage
                                
                                ```sh
                                # Display available commands and options
                                sql --help
                                
                                # Execute a query (table output by default)
                                sql query books.db 'from :books select title'
                                
                                # Execute a query and return JSON
                                sql query books.db 'from :books select title' -o json
                                
                                # Read a DSL statement from a file
                                sql query books.db --file examples/query.art
                                
                                # Compile a statement into PostgreSQL SQL
                                sql compile 'from :books select title' -d postgres
                                ```
                                
                                ### Commands
                                
                                - **`query`**: Executes a DSL statement against a SQLite database. Pass the expression directly, no `sql` statement needed.
                                - **`compile`**: Generates SQL text and bound parameters without connecting to a database.
                                
                                Statements are privided directly in the CLI, but you may also load from files using `--file`/`-f`.
                                
                                Files must contain a single statement using our DSL.
                                
                                Run `sql <command> --help` for additional options and usage details.
                                
                                ## Quick start
                                
                                Import the package, build a statement, and compile it:
                                
                                ```art
                                import {sql}!
                                
                                minimumAge: 18
                                statement: sql [
                                    from :users
                                    select [id name]
                                    where [age >= @minimumAge]
                                    order 'name
                                    limit 20
                                ]
                                
                                compiled: to :sqliteQuery statement
                                print compiled\text
                                inspect compiled\params
                                ```
                                
                                Inside `sql [...]`, names such as `age` refer to columns. Use `@minimumAge`
                                to capture an Arturo variable as a parameter value.
                                
                                `sql` returns an inspectable statement dictionary. Conversion gives you
                                `text` and `params` without executing the query. To target PostgreSQL instead,
                                use `to :postgresQuery statement`.
                                
                                ## Run a query
                                
                                Use `sql.query:` with an open SQLite connection:
                                
                                ```art
                                import {sql}!
                                
                                db: open.sqlite ":memory:"
                                failure: try [
                                    ignored: sql.query: db [create :books [column 'title :string]]
                                    ignored: sql.query: db [insert.columns: 'title :books -> row ["River Atlas"]]
                                    rows: sql.query: db [from :books select 'title]
                                    print tabular.header: ["Title"] rows
                                ]
                                close db
                                unless null? failure -> throw to :string failure
                                ```
                                
                                You own the connection. This pattern closes it even if a query fails, then
                                rethrows the error. For return values and formatting options, see
                                [execution and formatting](api/API.md#execution-and-formatting).
                                
                                ## Documentation
                                
                                | Looking for… | Read |
                                | --- | --- |
                                | Commands, arguments, and validation rules | [API reference](api/API.md) |
                                | Query layout and naming conventions | [Style guide](api/STYLE.md) |
                                | Examples of a particular SQL feature | [Feature catalog](api/README.md) |
                                | Dialect differences and driver output | [Compiled output](api/API.md#compiled-output) |
                                | Planned features and dialect support | [Roadmap](TODO.md) |
                                
                                For runnable scripts, browse [examples/](examples/). From a local checkout:
                                
                                ```sh
                                arturo src/main.art
                                arturo examples/sqlite.art
                                arturo examples/postgres.art
                                ```
                                
                                The PostgreSQL example compiles SQL without opening a database.
                                
                                ## Development
                                
                                From the repository root:
                                
                                ```sh
                                arturo -p install unitt
                                unitt
                                ```
                                
                                Use **Unitt 3.1.1+** and add `~/.arturo/packages/bin` to `PATH`.
                                CI runs the suite on Linux, macOS, FreeBSD, and Windows.
                                
                                The suite covers statement construction, compilation, SQLite execution, and
                                all feature catalog examples. The self-testing scripts in `examples/` provide
                                additional usage checks; `examples/api.art` uses an in-memory database.
                                
